    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d55">

                <pb facs="0074" n="60" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg60"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Pacific War/3855: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-24T09:00:00+07:00">April 24, 1944—9
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 24—1:47 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>714. Drumright reports in telegram April 20 that total of 17 American evacuees
                    from Honan had arrived Sian (Embassy’s 626, April 8, 6 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >1</hi>] p.m.). By despatch April 16<anchor n="34" xml:id="d55-f1"/> he
                    reports that women and children of 3 Americans at Fowyang, Anhwei, and 2 women
                    in Chengchow area were evacuating and expressed opinion that other missionaries
                    who had elected to remain are prepared to flee at a moment’s notice. Drumright
                    has informally notified Shensi provincial authorities of possible influx of
                    American refugees and has requested that facilities be accorded them.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
